ake this less likely", "Acute Inflammatory or Autoimmune Process: Elevated WBC could suggest infection or inflammation, but absence of localized infection signs and presence of blasts favor hematologic malignancy", "Endocrine Crisis: Hyperglycemia is ruled out by normal serum glucose, and hypoglycemia is absent"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional alarm signs (fever, night sweats, weight loss)", "Uncharacteristically high leukocyte count with blasts", "Systemic symptoms with multisystem involvement (hematologic + neurologic + metabolic)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0544346421162868, "posterior": 0.03216059624833023, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The combination of blasts in leukocytosis and systemic symptoms like fever and weight loss is more typical of myeloid neoplasms (e.g., AML) than chronic or lym…"], "evidence_against": ["The subacute onset and systemic symptoms (e.g., ataxia) are less typical of myeloid neoplasms but do not decisively exclude them."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.38918880577401876, "posterior": 0.1955850701962471, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The lack of lymphoid-specific features (e.g., skin changes) weakens the case for lymphoid neoplasms but does not exclude them entirely."], "evidence_agai